29	\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
30	\label{par:phase-segmentation}
31	Inspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \texttt{<think>} markers yields Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.887$ ($n{=}48$; Appendix~\ref{app:think-validation}). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.
32

tool_args.old_string:
DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B generates explicit \texttt{<think>}...\texttt{</think>} delimiters that demarcate chain-of-thought reasoning from final answers, providing a model-generated ground truth for reasoning phase boundaries. We evaluate whether our attention-sparsity-based phase segmentation (\S\ref{par:phase-segmentation}) recovers these boundaries on GSM8K ($n{=}48$ problems with valid phase assignments).

\paragraph{Token-level agreement.}
We binarize each token as ``thinking'' (inside \texttt{<think>}) or ``answer'' (outside) and compare against the sparsity-based low/high phase assignment. Cohen's $\kappa{=}0.887$ (median across problems), indicating near-perfect agreement between the data-driven sparsity proxy and the model's explicit reasoning markers. Of thinking tokens, $57.8\%$ fall in the low-sparsity phase; of answer tokens, $62.7\%$ fall in the high-sparsity phase.

\paragraph{Boundary proximity.}
The median distance between the sparsity-based phase boundary and the nearest \texttt{<think>}/\texttt{</think>} delimiter is 1 token, with $81.2\%$ of boundaries falling within 10 tokens. This tight alignment indicates that the attention sparsity transition closely tracks the model's self-identified shift from exploratory reasoning to structured answer generation.

\paragraph{Phase composition and II correlation.}
We examine whether the fraction of thinking tokens within each phase correlates with compound severity (II). At the mid-phase level, the thinking-token fraction correlates with II (Spearman $\rho{=}0.49$, $p < 0.001$), consistent with mid-phase segments that contain more reasoning content being more vulnerable to compound sparsity. Low- and high-phase correlations are non-significant, as expected: these phases are compositionally dominated by thinking or answer tokens, respectively, leaving insufficient variance for correlation.

\paragraph{Scope.}
This validation is limited to DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on GSM8K ($n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not produce explicit reasoning delimiters, precluding the same validation. Phase segmentation is computed on the dense model's attention patterns and is independent of sparsity level, so the agreement statistics are reported once rather than per-$s$.

tool_args.new_string:
DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B generates explicit \texttt{<think>}...\texttt{</think>} delimiters that demarcate chain-of-thought reasoning from final answers, providing a model-generated ground truth for reasoning phase boundaries. We evaluate whether our attention-sparsity-based phase segmentation (\S\ref{par:phase-segmentation}) recovers these boundaries on GSM8K ($n{=}48$ problems with valid phase assignments), using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation rather than a fixed global threshold.

\paragraph{Naming convention.}
A note on terminology: DeepSeek's \texttt{<think>} phase (active reasoning) exhibits \emph{low} attention sparsity---attention is broadly distributed across many positions during exploratory computation. Conversely, the answer phase exhibits \emph{high} attention sparsity---attention concentrates on a few positions during structured output. Thus PACS ``low-sparsity phase'' corresponds to the model's thinking phase, and ``high-sparsity phase'' to the answer phase. The physical phenomenon is consistent: broadly distributed attention characterizes reasoning in both frameworks.

\paragraph{Token-level agreement.}
We binarize each token as ``thinking'' (inside \texttt{<think>}) or ``answer'' (outside) and compare against the sparsity-based phase assignment. The adaptive segmentation achieves substantial agreement: $\kappa{=}0.88$ (per-problem mean $0.85$, median $0.88$), with token-level accuracy of $94.2\%$. Per-problem AUC-ROC is $0.98$ (median $0.99$), indicating near-perfect discriminability between thinking and answer phases based on attention sparsity alone. Of problems, $75\%$ achieve $\kappa \geq 0.80$ and $100\%$ achieve $\kappa \geq 0.60$. The \texttt{<think>} phase exhibits significantly lower attention sparsity than the answer phase ($0.656$ vs.\ $0.763$; Cohen's $d{=}-0.80$), confirming that attention distribution breadth reliably distinguishes reasoning from structured computation.

\paragraph{Boundary proximity.}
The median distance between the sparsity-based phase boundary and the nearest \texttt{<think>}/\texttt{</think>} delimiter is 1 token, with $81.2\%$ of boundaries falling within 10 tokens. This tight alignment indicates that the attention sparsity transition closely tracks the model's self-identified shift from exploratory reasoning to structured answer generation.

\paragraph{Phase composition and II correlation.}
We examine whether the fraction of thinking tokens within each phase correlates with compound severity (II). At the mid-phase level, the thinking-token fraction correlates with II (Spearman $\rho{=}0.49$, $p < 0.001$), consistent with mid-phase segments that contain more reasoning content being more vulnerable to compound sparsity. Low- and high-phase correlations are non-significant, as expected: these phases are compositionally dominated by thinking or answer tokens, respectively, leaving insufficient variance for correlation.

\paragraph{Scope.}
This validation is limited to DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B on GSM8K ($n{=}48$). Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct does not produce explicit reasoning delimiters, precluding the same validation. Phase segmentation is computed on the dense model's attention patterns and is independent of sparsity level, so the agreement statistics are reported once rather than per-$s$. A MATH-500 replication with dual-model validation is in progress; if results differ substantially, these GSM8K-based values will be updated.

